                "event": "Flood Watch",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Juneau AK",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 14 at 9:57PM AKDT until October 16 at 1:00PM AKDT by NWS Juneau AK",
                "description": "A stronger atmospheric river is expected to move over the panhandle\nbeginning early Thursday morning, continuing through Friday morning.\nMultiple waves moving up will bring persistent moderate to heavy rain\nfor much of the southern half of the panhandle. Elevated gusty winds\nare expected alongside this heavier rainfall on Thursday, with the\nstrongest winds expected Thursday night. Prolonged periods of\nmoderate to heavy rain will produce significant river rises with the\npotential for at least minor flooding.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The following areas, Central Inner Channels, Southern Inner\nChannels and Southeast Gulf Coast. This includes the cities of\nPetersburg, Kake, Port Alexander, Craig, Klawock, Hydaburg, Coffman\nCove, Thorne Bay, And Wrangell.\n\n* WHEN...From Thursday afternoon through Friday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, other low-lying areas and flood-prone locations.\nStreet flooding and flooding of creeks and rivers are possible.\nIsolated landslides are possible in areas of steep terrain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Rain totals between early Thursday morning and late Friday\nmorning for the Central Panhandle and Prince of Wales Island area\nare still expected to be around 2 to 3.5 inches, with higher rain\ntotals favoring southern facing mountain sides. Highest rain\nrates are currently expected during the late morning through\nevening timeframe on Thursday, where rates could be as high as\n0.25 inches per hour.\n\nConfidence has increased for the area seeing winds of 20 to 30\nmph with gusts to 40 mph on Thursday, with potential for up to 50\nmph wind gusts Thursday evening into the night. With the multiple\nrounds of moderate to heavy rainfall and the elevated winds\nThursday, there is possibility for isolated landslides along\nsteep mountain slopes.",
                "instruction": "Stay tuned to further developments by listening to your local radio,\ntelevision, or NOAA Weather Radio for further information.\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
                    "AWIPSidentifier": [
                        "FFAAJK"
                    ],
                    "WMOidentifier": [
                        "WGAK67 PAJK 150557"
                    ],
                    "NWSheadline": [
                        "FLOOD WATCH REMAINS IN EFFECT FROM THURSDAY AFTERNOON THROUGH FRIDAY AFTERNOON"
                    ],
